# Build an Ubuntu Core image
Create a bootable Ubuntu Core image from a model assertion.

## 1. Obtain or write the model assertion
You need a signed model assertion (or an unsigned one for testing).
Store credentials must be set up first:

  idiot env auth add <name>
  eval "$(idiot env auth login <name>)"

For a Dedicated Snap Store, also set:

  eval "$(idiot env store use <store-name>)"

## 2. Choose the target architecture
  eval "$(idiot env arch set amd64)"   # or arm64, armhf, etc.

Or use interactive selection: eval "$(idiot env arch set)"

## 3. Select the model
  idiot models pick

This clones / updates the canonical/models repo and lets you pick a model.

## 4. Build the image with ubuntu-image
  ubuntu-image snap <model-assertion.yaml>

For a signed assertion from the store:

  ubuntu-image snap --validation=ignore <model.assertion>

## 5. Inspect the resulting image
  idiot inspect image <pc.img>

Verify seeded snaps, model assertion, and that first boot state is as expected.

## 6. Flash to media
For USB / SD:
  sudo dd if=<image.img> of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress

For a VM:
  idiot vm run <image.img>

## 7. First boot validation
Boot the device and monitor console output.
After first boot completes, SSH in and verify:

  snap list
  snap changes
  snap connections
